T-1 GATE MET

Why section D is the one that matters
-------------------------------------

A liveness probe that fails when the database is unreachable gets the
agent killed by its orchestrator at exactly the moment the queue is doing
its job — and the restart discards the batch being assembled and every
batch in flight, which P1-7 measured as the whole of the node-loss RPO. A
recoverable outage becomes data loss because of the monitoring.

So `/healthz` reports **liveness only**: is the main loop still turning?
That is the one question a restart can answer. An outage surfaces as
`status: degraded` and `shipping: false` in the body, and as
`tributary_queue_bytes` climbing with `tributary_lines_shipped_total` flat
in the metrics — where an operator and an alert can see it, while the
process is left alone. Use `shipping` for a *readiness* probe if you want
traffic-shaping; do not wire it to liveness.

The drill proves the distinction rather than asserting it: it stops the
database container through the Docker socket, confirms 200/live/degraded
with 199,780 bytes spooled, then restarts it and waits for the queue to
drain to zero.

An earlier version of this drill left the stop optional and, with the
variable unset, silently checked a *healthy* agent — 12/12 green while
testing nothing. That is recorded because a green drill that exercises
nothing is the failure mode this whole bench directory exists to avoid.

The accounting invariant, checked from outside
----------------------------------------------

`DESIGN.md` §6.2 has always claimed the metric set is designed so that

    lines_read - lines_shipped - lines_quarantined == what is still held

is checkable externally. Section C now checks it against a live scrape
rather than trusting the claim. It also has a unit test
(`telemetry.rs::the_set_supports_the_accounting_invariant`) so the
property survives a refactor that drops one of the three terms.

What is exported
----------------

26 series, named as §6.2 specified them years before the endpoint existed.
The pipeline (read / shipped / quarantined / rejected / bisects / requests
/ unauthorized, plus the ship-vs-read seconds split that decides whether
L6's faster wire is worth building), the queue (bytes, segments, full,
spilled, drained), the P1-7 exposure (at_risk_lines, inflight_batches,
unread_bytes), the sources (files_open, files_lost, rotations,
pk_disambiguated, watermark_violations), and the L4 credential
(expiry_seconds, healthy, renewals_refused).

`tributary_credential_expiry_seconds` is the series to page on: it reports
-1 when no certificate is configured, deliberately distinct from 0, so an
agent that never had a certificate does not look like one whose
certificate just expired.

Cost
----

No new dependencies. hyper, hyper-util, http-body-util and bytes were
already compiled into the binary by reqwest, so serving two routes needed
only a direct declaration of what was already there — which matters for an
agent whose argument is that it is small enough to run on every node.

`[telemetry]` absent means no listener at all, the same additive posture
as `[output.tls]`: an agent that never configured it behaves exactly as it
did before, and no port is opened that nobody asked for. The example binds
127.0.0.1; a DaemonSet being scraped across the pod network needs
0.0.0.0, and that is a deliberate choice because the endpoint carries no
authentication and reports file paths and volumes.
